texprobe: a guest program for comparing texturing on a real Radeon and in
the emulator, texel by texel.

texprobe.c   Mac OS X 10.4 (PowerPC), GLUT.  Uploads mip-mapped textures in
             which every texel names its level, face and place, draws each
             level at one texel per pixel (GL_NEAREST_MIPMAP_NEAREST, so
             the card picks the level) and reads the pixels back.  One line
             per level in /tmp/texprobe.txt, PASS or FAIL with the first
             wrong texels.  Then replaces part of two levels with
             glTexSubImage2D and checks again ("sub").
               gcc -std=gnu99 -O2 -o texprobe texprobe.c \
                   -framework GLUT -framework OpenGL
               ./texprobe [stage=1|2] [only=ID] [hold=SECONDS] [wait=SECONDS]
             stage 1: 32-bit, 16-bit and DXT textures, among them chains
             with one-row levels many microtiles wide (1024x4), levels
             wider than a macrotile but not as tall (2048x8), and cubes.
             stage 2: 8-bit textures and two-byte luminance-alpha.
tags.py      Finds texprobe's levels in a capture of the emulator's VRAM
             (R300_CAP=prefix R300_CAP_TEX=1, then "echo 9000 > prefix.go"
             before texprobe starts) and compares where the driver put
             each level with the layout the emulator's sampler uses.

What the two say, and what they do not:
- texprobe.txt is a test of what comes out: on hardware it shows the
  driver, the card and the probe agree; in the emulator a FAIL is an
  emulator bug.  It says nothing about the order of texels inside a level
  in the card's memory: an upload and a sampler that both go through the
  same address translation give the same picture as none at all.
- tags.py reads the emulator's memory, which holds what the driver's
  upload commands put there as the emulator executes them (untiled).  It
  shows where the driver places each level and with what row pitch.  It
  is not a view of a real card's memory.

Results, 2026-10-12 (Tiger 10.4.11, ATI-1.4.18):
- PowerBook G4 (ATY,RV360M11): stage 1 486 of 486 levels PASS, stage 2
  161 of 161, no LOD bias needed.
- Emulator (Radeon 9700, gpu-work 5cc1c53): stage 1 every first upload
  PASS and every level where the layout expects it; 22 levels FAIL after
  glTexSubImage2D (32-bit: the whole texture's components change places;
  16-bit: the replaced texels are byte-swapped).  Stage 2: 93 of 161 FAIL,
  the 8-bit textures with rows under 32 bytes.
